    pr_ref:  # 2285
    description: "ADR-0017 carries TWO invalidation markers (its 2026-06-11 amendment and the\n[Invalidated by ADR-0037] banner above §3a) that say the same thing:\n\n    \"implementing fixes will re-key sink matching and refinement on dst_ref\"\n\nThat is unimplemented. Measured 2026-08-02: `dst_ref` appears 14 times in\nir.py and ZERO times in taint.py. Sink matching still string-parses the dst id:\n\n    _extract_callee_name(edge_dst)     # symbol_id.split(\":\")\n    _extract_callee_module(edge_dst)   # symbol_id.split(\":\")[1]\n\nADR-0037 made `dst_ref` unconditionally derived PRECISELY so consumers stop\nparsing `dst` strings, and folded the `unresolved` kind-slot token into\n`external_symbol`. Both taint predicates predate that and still read the string.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS BEYOND TIDINESS, with evidence from the same session:\n * `_extract_callee_module` returns `parts[1]`, which is a MODULE for an\n   external symbol and a FILE PATH for an in-repo one. Nothing in the name or\n   the signature says which. WI-damir's whole defect was a consumer treating\n   the path-shaped value as a module; the fix has to re-derive module-ness from\n   the path by stripping a file extension, which is a heuristic forced by the\n   string-parsing.\n * The 13 residual `net/http`+`Do` non-realizable matches are the same family:\n   the emitted module records the PACKAGE while the catalog records\n   PACKAGE.TYPE, so a consumer cannot verify the match by lookup. A typed\n   reference would carry that distinction instead of encoding it in a slot.\n\nSCOPE: taint.py's `_extract_callee_name` / `_extract_callee_module` and\ntaint_refine.py's dst module-segment REWRITES (which mutate the string in\nplace — the thing ADR-0037 most directly targets).\n\nDO NOT fold this into a bug fix. It is a contract migration with its own\nbefore/after: the cohort harness (scratchpad cohort-after.sh + ab2.py, 9 repos,\nper-flow diff) exists and should be run across it, because re-keying changes\nwhich edges match and the failure mode is silent."  # 2285
    fields: {}  # 2285
- op: update  # a9d2
  at: '2026-08-04T23:40:37Z'  # a9d2
  by: agent  # a9d2
  actor: jgstern_agent  # a9d2
  clock: 2  # a9d2
  nonce: a9d2  # a9d2
  set:  # a9d2
    title: "Re-key only the MODULE half of taint matching onto dst_ref; the NAME half must keep the string parse (measured: re-keying it drops 60.5% of taint call edges)"  # a9d2
    description: "RE-SCOPED 2026-08-04 after measurement. The original filing (below, kept) said\n\"re-key sink matching onto dst_ref\". I wrote that. It is wrong in a way that\nwould have caused a SILENT RECALL REGRESSION if implemented as stated, and the\ncorrection I recorded in the campaign plan (\"it can only serve the unresolved\narm; the benefit is roughly half what the filing says\") was ALSO wrong -- right\nabout the arm, wrong about the magnitude, and it missed the mechanism entirely.\nBoth are now replaced by a measurement.\n\n=== WHAT WAS MEASURED ===\n9-repo cohort (zoxide, express, SwiftyJSON, spring-petclinic, apollo-server,\ncaddy, pretix, alertmanager, kserve), full behavior maps, 13 languages.\nPopulation selected by production's own _is_taint_call_edge -- the exact filter\nboth propagate_taint_* loops use -- not a hand-written predicate (L53).\n95,950 taint call edges total.\n\n  is_resolved=True   33,089 (34.5%)  dst_ref is None for 100% BY CONSTRUCTION\n  unresolved +ref    37,972 (39.6%)  dst_ref populated\n  unresolved -ref    24,889 (25.9%)  dst_ref None\n\ndst_ref coverage on the unresolved arm is 60.4% overall and varies ~6x by\nlanguage: typescript 96.9% - bash/jsonnet 100% - go 72.6% - javascript 57.7% -\npython 50.7% - rust 34.1% - java 23.9% - swift 16.2%.\n\n=== THE THREE FACTS THAT RE-SCOPE THIS ===\n\n(1) THE RESOLVED ARM IS STRUCTURALLY OUT OF REACH, NOT MERELY UNCOVERED.\nfinalize.py:423-425 sets dst_ref=None on every is_resolved=True edge, and\nir.py:813 states the fourth cell (resolved + populated dst_ref) is NEVER\nproduced (ADR-0037 ruling 1). WI-damir's first-party gate operates on exactly\nthat population. No amount of producer work changes this; it is the design.\n\n(2) EVERY MISSING dst_ref IS ACCOUNTED FOR -- 24,889 of 24,889, in all 13\nlanguages. _derive_dst_ref_from_id (finalize.py:357-372) deliberately returns\nNone when the path slot is the \"external\" or \"<unknown>\" sentinel, because\nWI-huzuv forbids promoting a sentinel to a fabricated module path. There is no\nunexplained gap. (Note: ir.py:813 describes the None case as \"an unidentified\ndangling reference whose id cannot be parsed\". There are TWO None cases and the\ndominant one parses fine -- it simply carries no module. The docstring is\nimprecise, not wrong; worth a one-line correction when this is worked.)\n\n(3) THE ASYMMETRY, WHICH IS THE WHOLE POINT. On all 24,889 no-dst_ref edges,\nexactly one shape occurs: (_extract_callee_module, _module_from_symbol_path)\n== (\"external\", \"\"). So:\n\n  MODULE half: the string parse yields NOTHING USABLE on 24,889/24,889 (100%).\n               Re-keying onto dst_ref.module_path loses ZERO information and\n               gains a typed value on the 37,972 where it exists. Strictly\n               better. ADOPT.\n\n  NAME half:   the string parse yields a USABLE NAME on 24,889/24,889 (100%),\n               and it is the ONLY source of one there. Re-keying onto\n               dst_ref.name would silently drop 24,889 unresolved edges (25.9%\n               of the population) PLUS all 33,089 resolved ones -- 60.5% of all\n               taint call edges would stop matching. DO NOT ADOPT.\n\n=== THE ACTUAL SCOPE ===\n * Re-key the MODULE half only: taint.py:446's _extract_callee_module(edge_dst)\n   -> dst_ref.module_path when present, string parse otherwise (which returns\n   nothing usable anyway, so the fallback is a formality, not a hedge).\n * LEAVE _extract_callee_name (taint.py:403) as the name source for both arms.\n   It runs BEFORE the is_resolved branch and serves 100% of the population.\n   dst_ref.name may be used as a cross-check where present, never as the source.\n * taint_refine.py's dst module-segment rewrites are in scope for the same\n   reason as the module half (they mutate the string ADR-0037 wants retired).\n * The resolved arm keeps _module_from_symbol_path (taint.py:430). Not a\n   deferral -- there is no typed source to move it to.\n\n=== WHAT THIS DOES NOT FIX, AND WHY IT IS A SEPARATE ITEM ===\nThe 60.4% ceiling is a PRODUCER-side gap, not a taint one. java/rust/swift\nstamp the \"external\" sentinel in the path slot where typescript/go/bash stamp a\nreal module. Raising dst_ref coverage means fixing those analyzers' external-id\nemission, which is analyzer work with its own blast radius and belongs in its\nown item. Filing it here would repeat the scope-axis error this re-scope exists\nto correct (L1).\n\n=== ORIGINAL FILING, RETAINED ===\nADR-0017 carries TWO invalidation markers (its 2026-06-11 amendment and the\n[Invalidated by ADR-0037] banner above section 3a) that say the same thing:\n\"implementing fixes will re-key sink matching and refinement on dst_ref\".\nThat is unimplemented. Re-measured 2026-08-04 and STILL CURRENT: dst_ref appears\n14 times in ir.py, ZERO in taint.py, ZERO in taint_refine.py. Sink matching\nstill string-parses the dst id via _extract_callee_name / _extract_callee_module.\n\nThe original filing's two supporting arguments both survive and are the reason\nthe module half is worth doing:\n * _extract_callee_module returns parts[1], a MODULE for an external symbol and\n   a FILE PATH for an in-repo one, with nothing in the name or signature saying\n   which. WI-damir's whole defect was a consumer treating the path-shaped value\n   as a module.\n * the 13 residual net/http+Do non-realizable matches are the same family: the\n   emitted module records the PACKAGE while the catalog records PACKAGE.TYPE.\n\nDO NOT fold this into a bug fix. It is a contract migration with its own\nbefore/after: the cohort harness exists and should be run across it, because\nre-keying changes which edges match and the failure mode is silent. The\nnon-destructiveness clause matters here specifically (L57): the correct result\nfor the module re-key is that MATCH COUNTS DO NOT DROP, and a broken\nimplementation that drops matches will look like a precision improvement."  # a9d2
- op: discuss  # 86ff
  at: '2026-08-04T23:40:52Z'  # 86ff
  by: agent  # 86ff
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 86ff
  clock: 3  # 86ff
  nonce: 86ff  # 86ff
  message: "RE-SCOPED, not closed. Premise check on my own filing (L1, author-not-exempt) plus a 9-repo measurement using production classifiers. Three findings, in order of how much they change the work: (1) the resolved arm is structurally unreachable, not merely uncovered - finalize sets dst_ref=None on every is_resolved=True edge and ADR-0037 ruling 1 says that cell is never produced; (2) every one of the 24,889 missing dst_ref values is explained by the documented sentinel refusal (WI-huzuv), in all 13 languages, so there is no hidden gap to chase; (3) the decisive one - on that no-dst_ref population the string parse yields nothing usable for the MODULE (24,889 of 24,889) and a usable NAME for 24,889 of 24,889. So the module half is a strict improvement and the name half is a pure loss. Implementing the item AS FILED - re-key sink matching onto dst_ref - would have silently stopped 60.5 percent of taint call edges from matching, which for a security tool is the expensive direction and would have looked like a precision win. My earlier correction in the campaign plan said the benefit was roughly half the filing; that number was unmeasured and is superseded. The producer-side ceiling (java/rust/swift stamp the external sentinel where typescript/go/bash stamp a real module) is filed separately rather than folded in."  # 86ff
