- op: discuss  # ebd8
  at: '2026-08-05T00:59:11Z'  # ebd8
  by: agent  # ebd8
  actor: jgstern_agent  # ebd8
  clock: 5  # ebd8
  nonce: ebd8  # ebd8
  message: "DISSOLVED by PR #207, which fixed the underlying defect a different way. Recording the full arc because the item was wrong twice and the second time was my correction of the first. AS FILED: re-key sink matching onto dst_ref. Measured: that would have dropped 60.5 percent of taint call edges, because the NAME half of the parse is the only name source for the 24,889 unresolved edges with no dst_ref and for all 33,089 resolved ones. AS I CORRECTED IT this session: do the MODULE half only, which loses nothing and gains a typed value. That was true when written. Then the reason the module half was worth doing turned out to be a plain defect: _extract_callee_module took parts[1] on a colon-TOLERANT path slot, so it truncated std::fs to std, and since _lookup_named_entry rejects on a present-but-mismatched module rather than degrading, an edge naming its real module was REFUSED while an edge naming none MATCHED. Fixing that at the chokepoint (one right-anchored parser, four callers) took parse-vs-dst_ref divergence from 113 to 8 of 37,972 (0.021 percent), and in 6 of those 8 the dst_ref is the WRONG one (swift stamps a receiver expression). So the re-key now buys 8 edges and would make 6 worse. The ADR-0037 goal it was filed to serve - consumers stop parsing dst strings - is unreachable anyway: dst_ref is absent on 39.6 percent of the unresolved arm and on 100 percent of the resolved arm by construction, so the string parse cannot be retired. Closing wont_do rather than done because the literal remedy was never implemented and should not be. Two defects it exposed are filed separately."  # ebd8
